| `models.timeout_seconds.<adapter>` | number | `claude-code-headless` gets `600`; `openai-compatible` gets `60` | at least one entry, each an adapter name mapped to a number of seconds above 0 and at most `threading.TIMEOUT_MAX`, past which a thread wait overflows; a refusal names each entry it refuses | `models/limits.py` (`timeout_seconds`); `models/openai_compatible.py`; `models/claude_code_headless.py` | Sets how long one call of the adapter may take, counted from its start: `openai-compatible` gives its connect, a proxy's CONNECT tunnel, the TLS handshake, the send and every read of the reply only the time left, so a server that answers a byte at a time cannot stretch the call past the limit by more than one wake-up (`tests/integration/test_model_call_deadline.py`); the time running out while it connects or sends is `ModelUnavailable`, and while it reads the reply a `RuntimeError` naming the endpoint, the limit and this key. Name resolution is inside the limit, and each address of a host name with several waits only the time left. `claude-code-headless` sends its CLI's whole session SIGKILL at the limit and raises `ChildTimedOut`, a `RuntimeError` naming this key (`scripts/resource_limits.py`). A `timeout` argument to `openai-compatible` overrides it. An adapter that calls out and has no entry is a `config_loader.NotSet` naming the key and the adapter, never an unbounded call, and an empty map is refused when it is read. Absent, the adapter writes `[using default] models.timeout_seconds = {"claude-code-headless": 600, "openai-compatible": 60}` to stderr. | `fake`, which calls nothing; a live eval's call budget: `evals.live.max_calls` | always |
